# =============================================================================
# Lv-00 Axiom Package: Measure Theory (Abstract Measure & Integration)
# =============================================================================
#
# Corresponding Mathematical Theory:
#   Measure theory — the study of measures, σ-algebras, measurable spaces,
#   and integration. Founded by Émile Borel (1898), Henri Lebesgue (1902),
#   and Constantin Carathéodory (1914), measure theory generalizes the
#   classical notions of length, area, and volume to abstract sets. It is
#   the rigorous foundation for Lebesgue integration, probability theory,
#   and modern analysis.
#
# References:
#   - Lebesgue, H. (1902). "Intégrale, longueur, aire." Annali di
#     Matematica Pura ed Applicata, 7(1): 231-359.
#     https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02419242
#   - Carathéodory, C. (1914). "Über das lineare Maß von Punktmengen —
#     eine Verallgemeinerung des Längenbegriffs." Nachrichten von der
#     Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften zu Göttingen, 1914: 404-426.
#   - Halmos, P.R. (1950). "Measure Theory." D. Van Nostrand Co.
#     ISBN 0-387-90088-8. (Springer reprint 1974)
#   - Folland, G.B. (1999). "Real Analysis: Modern Techniques and Their
#     Applications" (2nd ed.). Wiley. ISBN 0-471-31716-0.
#   - Royden, H.L. and Fitzpatrick, P.M. (2010). "Real Analysis" (4th ed.).
#     Prentice Hall. ISBN 0-13-143747-X.
#   - Wikipedia: Measure (mathematics)
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Measure_(mathematics)
#   - Wikipedia: σ-algebra
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%CE%A3-algebra
#   - Wikipedia: Outer measure
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outer_measure
#   - Wikipedia: Carathéodory's extension theorem
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carath%C3%A9odory%27s_extension_theorem
#   - Wikipedia: Lebesgue measure
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebesgue_measure
#   - Wikipedia: Non-measurable set
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-measurable_set
#   - Wikipedia: Vitali set
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitali_set
#   - Wikipedia: Banach-Tarski paradox
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banach%E2%80%93Tarski_paradox
#   - nLab: Measure space
#     https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/measure+space
#   - nLab: Sigma-algebra
#     https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/sigma-algebra
#   - Dudley, R.M. (2002). "Real Analysis and Probability." Cambridge
#     University Press. ISBN 0-521-00754-3.
#
# Primitive Notions:
#   - Set X (the underlying point set / sample space)
#   - σ-algebra Σ over X (the collection of measurable subsets)
#   - Measure function μ: Σ → [0, +∞] (the set function assigning "size")
#   - Outer measure μ*: 2^X → [0, +∞] (defined on all subsets)
#
# Core Axioms:
#
#   I. σ-Algebra Axioms (for the measurable space (X, Σ)):
#     SA1. X ∈ Σ (contains the whole space)
#     SA2. If A ∈ Σ, then Aᶜ ∈ Σ (closed under complement)
#     SA3. If A₁, A₂, ... ∈ Σ, then ∪ᵢ Aᵢ ∈ Σ (closed under countable unions)
#
#   II. Measure Axioms (for the measure space (X, Σ, μ)):
#     M1. μ(∅) = 0 (null empty set)
#     M2. Non-negativity: ∀E ∈ Σ, μ(E) ≥ 0
#     M3. Countable additivity (σ-additivity):
#         For pairwise disjoint {Eₖ} ⊆ Σ:
#         μ(∪ₖ Eₖ) = Σₖ μ(Eₖ)
#
#   III. Outer Measure Axioms (Carathéodory, for μ*: 2^X → [0, +∞]):
#     OM1. μ*(∅) = 0 (null empty set)
#     OM2. Monotonicity: A ⊆ B ⟹ μ*(A) ≤ μ*(B)
#     OM3. Countable subadditivity:
#         μ*(∪ⱼ Bⱼ) ≤ Σⱼ μ*(Bⱼ)
#
#   IV. Carathéodory Measurability Condition:
#     A set E ⊆ X is μ*-measurable iff:
#     ∀A ⊆ X: μ*(A) = μ*(A ∩ E) + μ*(A ∩ Eᶜ)
#
# Key Derived Concepts Formalized as Templates:
#   - Measurable space, measure space, probability space
#   - Null sets, complete measures, completion of a measure
#   - σ-finite measures, semifinite measures, localizable measures
#   - Signed measures, Jordan decomposition (Hahn decomposition)
#   - Complex measures, total variation measure
#   - Product measures (Fubini-Tonelli construction)
#   - Lebesgue measure on ℝⁿ
#   - Borel σ-algebra, Lebesgue σ-algebra
#   - Pushforward measures, restriction of measures
#   - Absolutely continuous measures, Radon-Nikodym derivative
#   - Singular measures, Lebesgue decomposition
#   - Carathéodory extension theorem (pre-measure → measure)
#   - Dynkin's π-λ theorem
#   - Monotone convergence, Fatou's lemma, dominated convergence
#   - Lᵖ spaces (as measure-theoretic constructions)
#   - Hausdorff measure, Hausdorff dimension
#
# Known Undecidable / Unconstructible Problems:
#   - Existence of non-measurable sets (requires Axiom of Choice)
#   - Lebesgue measure of a Vitali set (non-measurable)
#   - Banach-Tarski paradox (non-measurable decomposition)
#   - Measurability of all subsets of ℝ (inconsistent with ZFC)
#   - Classification of measures up to isomorphism (undecidable)
#   - Is every set measurable? (independent of ZF without Choice)
#   - Existence of a universal measure (Solovay model)
#   - Computing Hausdorff dimension of general sets (undecidable)
#
# Relationship to Other Lv-00 Axiom Packages:
#   - real_analysis: measure theory extends real analysis with Lebesgue
#     integration; ℝ with Lebesgue measure is the canonical example
#   - zfc_set_theory: the existence of non-measurable sets depends on
#     the Axiom of Choice; Solovay's model shows consistency of "all
#     sets are Lebesgue measurable" with ZF + Dependent Choice
#   - point_set_topology: the Borel σ-algebra is generated by open sets;
#     Radon measures bridge measure theory and topology
#   - functional_analysis: Lᵖ spaces are fundamental Banach/Hilbert
#     spaces built on measure theory; spectral measures in operator theory
#   - category_theory: the category of measure spaces with measurable maps
#   - probability theory (future): a probability measure is a measure
#     with total mass 1; Kolmogorov's axioms are measure-theoretic
#
# Author: Lv-00 Axiom Package System (AI-Discovered)
# Date: 2026-05-24
# Version: 1.0.0
# =============================================================================

axiom "measure_theory" "1.0.0" {

    # =================================================================
    # Constraint Templates
    # =================================================================
    # Each template represents a fundamental axiom, property, or
    # construction in measure theory.
    #
    # Parameter semantics:
    #   Points are elements of the underlying set X.
    #   Regions are measurable subsets of X (elements of Σ).
    #   Scalars are non-negative extended real numbers [0, +∞].
    # =================================================================

    # --- Group I: σ-Algebra Axioms (Measurable Space Structure) ---

    # SA1. The whole space X is measurable:
    #   X ∈ Σ
    # Params: (no parameters — this is a global property of the space)
    template "sigma_algebra_contains_X" 0

    # SA2. Closure under complement:
    #   If A ∈ Σ, then Aᶜ = X \ A ∈ Σ.
    # Params: measurable set A -> complement measurable set Aᶜ
    template "sigma_algebra_complement" 1

    # SA3. Closure under countable unions:
    #   If A₁, A₂, A₃, ... ∈ Σ, then ∪ᵢ₌₁^∞ Aᵢ ∈ Σ.
    # Params: countable family of measurable sets -> union measurable set
    template "sigma_algebra_countable_union" 1

    # SA4. Closure under countable intersections (derived from SA2 + SA3):
    #   If A₁, A₂, ... ∈ Σ, then ∩ᵢ₌₁^∞ Aᵢ ∈ Σ.
    # Params: countable family of measurable sets -> intersection measurable set
    template "sigma_algebra_countable_intersection" 1

    # SA5. The empty set is measurable (derived from SA1 + SA2):
    #   ∅ = Xᶜ ∈ Σ
    # Params: (no parameters)
    template "sigma_algebra_contains_empty" 0

    # SA6. Closure under set difference (derived):
    #   A, B ∈ Σ ⟹ A \ B = A ∩ Bᶜ ∈ Σ
    # Params: measurable set A, measurable set B -> difference A \ B
    template "sigma_algebra_set_difference" 2

    # SA7. Symmetric difference (derived):
    #   A △ B = (A \ B) ∪ (B \ A) ∈ Σ
    # Params: measurable set A, measurable set B -> symmetric difference
    template "sigma_algebra_symmetric_difference" 2

    # --- Group II: Core Measure Axioms ---

    # M1. Null empty set:
    #   μ(∅) = 0
    # Params: (no parameters)
    template "measure_null_empty_set" 0

    # M2. Non-negativity of measure:
    #   ∀E ∈ Σ, μ(E) ≥ 0
    # Params: measurable set E -> non-negative scalar μ(E)
    template "measure_non_negativity" 1

    # M3. Countable additivity (σ-additivity):
    #   For pairwise disjoint {Eₖ} ⊆ Σ:
    #   μ(∪ₖ Eₖ) = Σₖ μ(Eₖ)
    # Params: countable family of pairwise disjoint measurable sets
    template "measure_countable_additivity" 1

    # M4. Finite additivity (derived from M3):
    #   A ∩ B = ∅ ⟹ μ(A ∪ B) = μ(A) + μ(B)
    # Params: disjoint measurable set A, disjoint measurable set B
    template "measure_finite_additivity" 2

    # M5. Monotonicity (derived from M2 + M3):
    #   A ⊆ B ⟹ μ(A) ≤ μ(B)
    # Params: measurable set A (subset), measurable set B (superset)
    template "measure_monotonicity" 2

    # M6. Countable subadditivity (derived from M3):
    #   μ(∪ₖ Eₖ) ≤ Σₖ μ(Eₖ) (not necessarily disjoint)
    # Params: countable family of measurable sets
    template "measure_countable_subadditivity" 1

    # M7. Continuity from below:
    #   E₁ ⊆ E₂ ⊆ ... ⟹ μ(∪ₖ Eₖ) = limₖ μ(Eₖ)
    # Params: increasing sequence of measurable sets
    template "measure_continuity_from_below" 1

    # M8. Continuity from above (requires μ(E₁) < ∞):
    #   E₁ ⊇ E₂ ⊇ ..., μ(E₁) < ∞ ⟹ μ(∩ₖ Eₖ) = limₖ μ(Eₖ)
    # Params: decreasing sequence of measurable sets with finite first measure
    template "measure_continuity_from_above" 1

    # --- Group III: Outer Measure Axioms (Carathéodory) ---

    # OM1. Null empty set for outer measure:
    #   μ*(∅) = 0
    # Params: (no parameters)
    template "outer_measure_null_empty" 0

    # OM2. Monotonicity of outer measure:
    #   A ⊆ B ⟹ μ*(A) ≤ μ*(B)
    # Params: set A (subset), set B (superset)
    template "outer_measure_monotonicity" 2

    # OM3. Countable subadditivity of outer measure:
    #   μ*(∪ⱼ Bⱼ) ≤ Σⱼ μ*(Bⱼ)
    # Params: countable family of arbitrary subsets
    template "outer_measure_countable_subadditivity" 1

    # --- Group IV: Carathéodory Measurability ---

    # CM1. Carathéodory measurability condition:
    #   E is μ*-measurable iff ∀A ⊆ X:
    #   μ*(A) = μ*(A ∩ E) + μ*(A ∩ Eᶜ)
    # Params: candidate set E, test set A
    template "caratheodory_measurability" 2

    # CM2. The measurable sets form a σ-algebra:
    #   The collection M of all μ*-measurable sets is a σ-algebra.
    # Params: (no parameters — structural theorem)
    template "measurable_sets_form_sigma_algebra" 0

    # CM3. μ* restricted to M is a complete measure:
    #   The restriction of μ* to the σ-algebra M of measurable sets
    #   yields a complete measure.
    # Params: (no parameters — structural theorem)
    template "outer_measure_restriction_complete" 0

    # --- Group V: Null Sets and Completeness ---

    # NS1. Null set definition:
    #   N is a null set if N ∈ Σ and μ(N) = 0.
    # Params: measurable set N -> null set property
    template "null_set" 1

    # NS2. Subsets of null sets (completeness):
    #   If N is a null set and E ⊆ N, then E is measurable with μ(E) = 0.
    # Params: null set N, subset E
    template "null_set_subset_measurable" 2

    # NS3. Almost everywhere (a.e.) property:
    #   A property holds μ-almost everywhere if it fails only on a null set.
    # Params: measurable set where property fails -> null set
    template "almost_everywhere" 1

    # NS4. Completion of a measure space:
    #   (X, Σ̄, μ̄) where Σ̄ = {E ⊆ X : ∃A, B ∈ Σ, A ⊆ E ⊆ B, μ(B\A) = 0}
    # Params: original measurable space -> completed measurable space
    template "measure_completion" 1

    # --- Group VI: σ-Finite and Semifinite Measures ---

    # SF1. σ-finite measure:
    #   X = ∪ₖ Xₖ where each μ(Xₖ) < ∞.
    # Params: countable exhaustion of X by finite-measure sets
    template "sigma_finite_measure" 1

    # SF2. Semifinite measure:
    #   If μ(E) = ∞, then ∃F ⊆ E with 0 < μ(F) < ∞.
    # Params: set E with infinite measure -> finite-measure subset F
    template "semifinite_measure" 2

    # SF3. Localizable measure (generalization of σ-finite):
    #   Supports essential supremum of families of measurable sets.
    # Params: family of measurable sets -> essential supremum
    template "localizable_measure" 1

    # --- Group VII: Signed and Complex Measures ---

    # SM1. Signed measure definition:
    #   ν: Σ → [-∞, +∞] with ν(∅) = 0 and countable additivity.
    # Params: signed measure ν on measurable set
    template "signed_measure" 1

    # SM2. Hahn decomposition theorem:
    #   X = P ∪ N, P ∩ N = ∅, ν(E ∩ P) ≥ 0, ν(E ∩ N) ≤ 0 for all E ∈ Σ.
    # Params: signed measure ν -> positive set P, negative set N
    template "hahn_decomposition" 1

    # SM3. Jordan decomposition:
    #   ν = ν⁺ - ν⁻ where ν⁺, ν⁻ are mutually singular positive measures.
    # Params: signed measure ν -> positive variation ν⁺, negative variation ν⁻
    template "jordan_decomposition" 1

    # SM4. Total variation measure:
    #   |ν| = ν⁺ + ν⁻
    # Params: signed measure ν -> total variation |ν|
    template "total_variation_measure" 1

    # SM5. Complex measure:
    #   μ: Σ → ℂ with countable additivity and |μ|(X) < ∞.
    # Params: complex measure μ on measurable set
    template "complex_measure" 1

    # --- Group VIII: Absolute Continuity and the Radon-Nikodym Theorem ---

    # AC1. Absolute continuity of measures:
    #   ν ≪ μ iff μ(E) = 0 ⟹ ν(E) = 0.
    # Params: measure ν, measure μ -> absolute continuity relation
    template "absolute_continuity" 2

    # AC2. Mutually singular measures:
    #   μ ⊥ ν iff ∃E ∈ Σ: μ(E) = 0 and ν(Eᶜ) = 0.
    # Params: measure μ, measure ν -> singular decomposition set E
    template "mutual_singularity" 2

    # AC3. Radon-Nikodym theorem:
    #   If ν ≪ μ and μ is σ-finite, then ∃f (Radon-Nikodym derivative)
    #   such that ν(E) = ∫_E f dμ for all E ∈ Σ.
    # Params: absolutely continuous measure ν, reference measure μ -> density f
    template "radon_nikodym_theorem" 2

    # AC4. Lebesgue decomposition theorem:
    #   ν = ν_ac + ν_s where ν_ac ≪ μ and ν_s ⊥ μ.
    # Params: measure ν, reference measure μ -> ac part, singular part
    template "lebesgue_decomposition" 2

    # --- Group IX: Product Measures and Fubini-Tonelli ---

    # PM1. Product σ-algebra:
    #   Σ₁ ⊗ Σ₂ is the σ-algebra generated by measurable rectangles
    #   A × B where A ∈ Σ₁, B ∈ Σ₂.
    # Params: σ-algebra Σ₁, σ-algebra Σ₂ -> product σ-algebra
    template "product_sigma_algebra" 2

    # PM2. Product measure construction:
    #   (μ₁ × μ₂)(E) = ∫ μ₂(E_x) dμ₁(x) for E ∈ Σ₁ ⊗ Σ₂.
    # Params: measure μ₁, measure μ₂ -> product measure
    template "product_measure" 2

    # PM3. Fubini's theorem:
    #   If f is Σ₁⊗Σ₂-integrable, then the iterated integrals are equal:
    #   ∫∫ f dμ₁ dμ₂ = ∫∫ f dμ₂ dμ₁.
    # Params: integrable function f, measure μ₁, measure μ₂
    template "fubini_theorem" 3

    # PM4. Tonelli's theorem (for non-negative functions):
    #   If f ≥ 0 is measurable, iterated integrals equal the double integral.
    # Params: non-negative measurable function f, measure μ₁, measure μ₂
    template "tonelli_theorem" 3

    # --- Group X: Convergence Theorems ---

    # CT1. Monotone Convergence Theorem (Beppo Levi):
    #   0 ≤ f₁ ≤ f₂ ≤ ... → f a.e. ⟹ ∫fₖ dμ → ∫f dμ.
    # Params: increasing sequence of non-negative measurable functions
    template "monotone_convergence_theorem" 1

    # CT2. Fatou's Lemma:
    #   ∫lim inf fₖ dμ ≤ lim inf ∫fₖ dμ (for non-negative fₖ).
    # Params: sequence of non-negative measurable functions
    template "fatou_lemma" 1

    # CT3. Dominated Convergence Theorem (Lebesgue):
    #   fₖ → f a.e., |fₖ| ≤ g, g ∈ L¹ ⟹ ∫fₖ → ∫f.
    # Params: sequence of functions, dominating function g
    template "dominated_convergence_theorem" 2

    # CT4. Uniform integrability:
    #   A family {fₐ} is uniformly integrable if
    #   lim_{μ(E)→0} sup_α ∫_E |fₐ| dμ = 0.
    # Params: family of integrable functions
    template "uniform_integrability" 1

    # --- Group XI: Carathéodory Extension Theorem ---

    # CE1. Pre-measure on a ring:
    #   μ₀: R → [0, +∞] is a pre-measure if it is σ-additive on R.
    # Params: ring of sets R, pre-measure μ₀
    template "pre_measure" 2

    # CE2. Carathéodory extension (outer measure from pre-measure):
    #   μ*(E) = inf{Σₖ μ₀(Aₖ) : E ⊆ ∪ₖ Aₖ, Aₖ ∈ R}
    # Params: pre-measure μ₀ on ring R -> outer measure μ*
    template "caratheodory_outer_measure_construction" 1

    # CE3. Carathéodory extension theorem:
    #   A σ-finite pre-measure on a ring extends uniquely to a measure
    #   on the generated σ-algebra.
    # Params: pre-measure μ₀ on ring R -> unique measure on σ(R)
    template "caratheodory_extension_theorem" 1

    # CE4. Dynkin's π-λ theorem:
    #   If a π-system P is contained in a λ-system L, then σ(P) ⊆ L.
    #   Used to prove uniqueness of measure extensions.
    # Params: π-system P, λ-system L
    template "dynkin_pi_lambda_theorem" 2

    # --- Group XII: Specific Measures ---

    # SP1. Counting measure:
    #   μ(E) = |E| (cardinality of E), ∞ if E is infinite.
    # Params: set E -> cardinality measure
    template "counting_measure" 1

    # SP2. Dirac (point) measure:
    #   δₓ(E) = 1 if x ∈ E, 0 otherwise.
    # Params: point x, measurable set E -> indicator value
    template "dirac_measure" 2

    # SP3. Lebesgue measure on ℝ:
    #   m([a,b]) = b - a, extended to the Lebesgue σ-algebra.
    # Params: interval [a,b] -> length b - a
    template "lebesgue_measure_interval" 2

    # SP4. Lebesgue measure on ℝⁿ:
    #   m(Q) = ∏ᵢ(bᵢ - aᵢ) for a rectangle Q = ∏[aᵢ, bᵢ].
    # Params: n-dimensional rectangle -> n-dimensional volume
    template "lebesgue_measure_rn" 2

    # SP5. Borel σ-algebra:
    #   The σ-algebra generated by open sets of a topological space.
    # Params: topological space X -> Borel σ-algebra B(X)
    template "borel_sigma_algebra" 1

    # SP6. Hausdorff measure (generalized measure for dimension):
    #   H^s_δ(E) = inf{Σᵢ (diam Uᵢ)^s : E ⊆ ∪ᵢ Uᵢ, diam Uᵢ < δ}
    # Params: set E, dimension parameter s, approximation scale δ -> measure
    template "hausdorff_measure" 3

    # SP7. Hausdorff dimension:
    #   dim_H(E) = inf{s ≥ 0 : H^s(E) = 0} = sup{s ≥ 0 : H^s(E) = ∞}
    # Params: set E -> Hausdorff dimension
    template "hausdorff_dimension" 1

    # SP8. Pushforward (image) measure:
    #   (f_*μ)(B) = μ(f⁻¹(B)) for measurable f: X → Y.
    # Params: measurable function f, measure μ on X -> pushforward measure on Y
    template "pushforward_measure" 2

    # SP9. Restriction of a measure:
    #   μ|_A(E) = μ(E ∩ A) for A ∈ Σ.
    # Params: measure μ, measurable set A -> restricted measure
    template "measure_restriction" 2

    # --- Group XIII: Lᵖ Spaces (Measure-Theoretic Construction) ---

    # LP1. Lᵖ norm:
    #   ‖f‖_p = (∫|f|^p dμ)^{1/p} for 1 ≤ p < ∞.
    # Params: measurable function f, exponent p -> Lᵖ norm
    template "lp_norm" 2

    # LP2. L^∞ norm (essential supremum):
    #   ‖f‖_∞ = ess sup|f| = inf{M ≥ 0 : |f| ≤ M a.e.}
    # Params: measurable function f -> essential supremum
    template "linf_norm" 1

    # LP3. Hölder's inequality:
    #   ∫|fg| dμ ≤ ‖f‖_p · ‖g‖_q where 1/p + 1/q = 1.
    # Params: function f, function g, conjugate exponents p, q
    template "holder_inequality" 4

    # LP4. Minkowski's inequality (triangle inequality for Lᵖ):
    #   ‖f + g‖_p ≤ ‖f‖_p + ‖g‖_p
    # Params: function f, function g, exponent p
    template "minkowski_inequality" 3

    # LP5. Jensen's inequality (for convex functions):
    #   φ(∫f dμ) ≤ ∫φ(f) dμ for convex φ, when μ(X) = 1.
    # Params: convex function φ, integrable function f, probability measure μ
    template "jensen_inequality" 3

    # --- Group XIV: Probability Measure (Special Case) ---

    # PR1. Probability measure:
    #   A measure P with P(X) = 1.
    # Params: measure space -> probability measure
    template "probability_measure" 1

    # PR2. Conditional expectation:
    #   E[X | 𝒢] is the 𝒢-measurable r.v. such that
    #   ∫_G X dP = ∫_G E[X | 𝒢] dP for all G ∈ 𝒢.
    # Params: integrable random variable X, sub-σ-algebra 𝒢 -> conditional expectation
    template "conditional_expectation" 2

    # PR3. Independence of σ-algebras:
    #   𝒜 and ℬ are independent iff P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B) for all A ∈ 𝒜, B ∈ ℬ.
    # Params: σ-algebra 𝒜, σ-algebra ℬ -> independence relation
    template "sigma_algebra_independence" 2

    # =================================================================
    # Known Unconstructible / Undecidable Problems
    # =================================================================

    # Vitali set: a subset of ℝ that is not Lebesgue measurable.
    # Its existence requires the Axiom of Choice. Under ZF+DC (Solovay's
    # model), all subsets of ℝ can be Lebesgue measurable.
    unconstructible "vitali_set_non_measurable" {
        reduces_to "axiom_of_choice"
        dependency "zfc_set_theory"
        dependency "lebesgue_measure_rn"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitali_set"
        green_verified true
    }

    # Banach-Tarski paradox: a solid ball in ℝ³ can be decomposed into
    # a finite number of non-measurable pieces and reassembled into two
    # identical copies. This is possible because Lebesgue measure cannot
    # be extended to all subsets of ℝ³ in a rotation-invariant way.
    unconstructible "banach_tarski_paradox" {
        reduces_to "non_measurable_decomposition"
        dependency "zfc_set_theory"
        dependency "lebesgue_measure_rn"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banach%E2%80%93Tarski_paradox"
        green_verified true
    }

    # Universal measure problem: does there exist a countably additive,
    # translation-invariant measure on all subsets of ℝⁿ that extends
    # Lebesgue measure? Answer: NO (for n ≥ 3), and NO for n ≥ 1
    # if we require translation invariance and normalization.
    unconstructible "universal_measure_on_all_subsets" {
        reduces_to "non_measurable_set_existence"
        dependency "zfc_set_theory"
        dependency "lebesgue_measure_rn"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-measurable_set"
        green_verified true
    }

    # Solovay's model: In ZF + Dependent Choice, it is consistent that
    # all subsets of ℝ are Lebesgue measurable. This shows that the
    # existence of non-measurable sets cannot be proved in ZF+DC alone.
    unconstructible "all_sets_measurable_consistency" {
        reduces_to "independence_from_ZF_DC"
        dependency "zfc_set_theory"
        dependency "measure_null_empty_set"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solovay_model"
        green_verified true
    }

    # Hausdorff dimension computation: computing the exact Hausdorff
    # dimension of general (fractal) sets is algorithmically undecidable.
    unconstructible "hausdorff_dimension_computation" {
        reduces_to "algorithmic_undecidability"
        dependency "computability_theory"
        dependency "hausdorff_measure"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hausdorff_dimension"
        green_verified false
    }

    # Classification of measure spaces up to isomorphism:
    # Two measure spaces are isomorphic if there exists a bijection
    # preserving the measurable structure and measure. The general
    # classification problem is undecidable.
    unconstructible "measure_space_isomorphism_classification" {
        reduces_to "isomorphism_problem"
        dependency "zfc_set_theory"
        dependency "measure_countable_additivity"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_probability_space"
        green_verified false
    }

    # Measure extension uniqueness without σ-finiteness:
    # A pre-measure on a ring may have multiple extensions to the
    # generated σ-algebra if it is not σ-finite.
    unconstructible "measure_extension_uniqueness_without_sigma_finite" {
        reduces_to "non_uniqueness_of_extension"
        dependency "caratheodory_extension_theorem"
        dependency "sigma_finite_measure"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carath%C3%A9odory%27s_extension_theorem"
        green_verified true
    }

    # Riemann-integrability characterization:
    # Deciding whether a bounded function on [a,b] is Riemann integrable
    # requires checking that the set of discontinuities has Lebesgue
    # measure zero — this is itself a non-trivial measure-theoretic problem.
    unconstructible "riemann_integrability_decision" {
        reduces_to "lebesgue_measure_zero_set"
        dependency "lebesgue_measure_rn"
        dependency "null_set"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_integral#Integrability"
        green_verified true
    }

    # =================================================================
    # Logical Framework
    # =================================================================

    bottom_geometry "measure_space_extended_reals"
    negation_encoding "classical_equality"
    contradiction_behavior "explosion_principle"
}
